{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChU1NJx1SwxXD9fuzwuCS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0181UjBBg1EEk1yREP6559qm","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /work/internal/initcmd; python3 - <<'E'\ns=open('initcmd_test.go').read()\ni=s.index('\tt.Run(\"checkAuth\"'); j=s.index('\tt.Run(\"no .git at root\"')\ns=s[:i]+s[j:]\ns=s.replace(\"initBody(Result{}, res)\",\"initBody(Result{}, res, github.AuthApp)\")\ns+='''\nfunc TestInitBodyAuthKinds(t *testing.T) {\n\tres, err := loadStarter(t, nil)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tapp := initBody(Result{}, res, github.AuthApp)\n\ttok := initBody(Result{}, res, github.AuthToken)\n\tfor name, body := range map[string]string{\"app\": app, \"token\": tok} {\n\t\tfor _, want := range []string{\"## Follow-up ard does not do\", \"ard never writes `.github/`\", \"bot-commits.yml\", \"ARD_BOT_LOGIN\"} {\n\t\t\tif !strings.Contains(body, want) {\n\t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"%s body lacks %q\", name, want)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif !strings.Contains(app, \"App has no permission\") || !strings.Contains(app, \"<slug>[bot]\") {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"app body:\\\\n%s\", app)\n\t}\n\tif strings.Contains(tok, \"no permission\") || strings.Contains(tok, \"<slug>[bot]\") || !strings.Contains(tok, \"token's user\") {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"token body:\\\\n%s\", tok)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestResolveAuth(t *testing.T) {\n\tkey, err := rsa.GenerateKey(rand.Reader, 2048)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tpemKey := string(pem.EncodeToMemory(&pem.Block{Type: \"RSA PRIVATE KEY\", Bytes: x509.MarshalPKCS1PrivateKey(key)}))\n\tsecret := \"tok-\" + strings.Repeat(\"x\", 12)\n\tfound := func(string) (string, error) { return \"/bin/gh\", nil }\n\tmissing := func(string) (string, error) { return \"\", errors.New(\"not found\") }\n\tghOK := func(_ context.Context, _ string, args ...string) ([]byte, error) {\n\t\tif len(args) > 1 && args[1] == \"token\" {\n\t\t\treturn []byte(secret + \"\\\\n\"), nil\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn nil, nil\n\t}\n\tghOut := func(context.Context, string, ...string) ([]byte, error) { return nil, errors.New(\"not logged in\") }\n\ttests := []struct {\n\t\tname     string\n\t\tenv      map[string]string\n\t\tlook     func(string) (string, error)\n\t\trun      github.RunFunc\n\t\twantKind github.AuthKind\n\t\twantErr  error\n\t\tanyErr   bool\n\t}{\n\t\t{name: \"app\", env: map[string]string{\"ARD_GITHUB_APP_ID\": \"1\", \"ARD_GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID\": \"2\", \"ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY\": pemKey}, look: missing, wantKind: github.AuthApp},\n\t\t{name: \"env token\", env: map[string]string{\"ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN\": secret}, look: found, wantKind: github.AuthToken},\n\t\t{name: \"gh fallback\", look: found, run: ghOK, wantKind: github.AuthToken},\n\t\t{name: \"no gh\", look: missing, wantErr: github.ErrNoGh},\n\t\t{name: \"gh not logged in\", look: found, run: ghOut, wantErr: github.ErrGhNotLoggedIn},\n\t\t{name: \"both\", env: map[string]string{\"ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN\": secret, \"ARD_GITHUB_APP_ID\": \"1\"}, look: found, anyErr: true},\n\t}\n\tfor _, tt := range tests {\n\t\tt.Run(tt.name, func(t *testing.T) {\n\t\t\tfor _, k := range []string{\"ARD_GITHUB_APP_ID\", \"ARD_GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID\", \"ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY\", \"ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE\", \"ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN\"} {\n\t\t\t\tt.Setenv(k, tt.env[k])\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\toldLook, oldRun := lookPath, runGh\n\t\t\tt.Cleanup(func() { lookPath, runGh = oldLook, oldRun })\n\t\t\tghCalled := false\n\t\t\tlookPath = tt.look\n\t\t\trunGh = func(ctx context.Context, n string, a ...string) ([]byte, error) {\n\t\t\t\tghCalled = true\n\t\t\t\treturn tt.run(ctx, n, a...)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\te := &cli.Env{Stderr: io.Discard}\n\t\t\tauth, err := resolveAuth(context.Background(), e)\n\t\t\tif tt.wantErr != nil || tt.anyErr {\n\t\t\t\tif err == nil || (tt.wantErr != nil && !errors.Is(err, tt.wantErr)) {\n\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"err = %v\", err)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tif strings.Contains(err.Error(), secret) {\n\t\t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"error contains token: %v\", err)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\treturn\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif auth.Kind() != tt.wantKind {\n\t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"kind = %v\", auth.Kind())\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif ghCalled != (tt.name == \"gh fallback\") {\n\t\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"gh called = %v\", ghCalled)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t})\n\t}\n}\n'''\ns=s.replace('import (','import (\\n\\t\"crypto/rand\"\\n\\t\"crypto/rsa\"\\n\\t\"crypto/x509\"\\n\\t\"encoding/pem\"',1)\nopen('initcmd_test.go','w').write(s)\nE\ngofmt -w initcmd_test.go; cd ../..; go vet ./internal/initcmd && go test ./internal/initcmd 2>&1 | tail -20; grep -n \"App only\\|only.*App\\|dry-run only\\|DRY.*token\" -i docs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md docs/proposal.md docs/site/getting-started.md docs/layout.md | head -20"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2544,"cache_read_input_tokens":24210,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2544},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"722ce48d-201b-495f-b707-ef0110c1be2a","uuid":"183a1833-f465-4272-aee7-dff344e72b37","timestamp":"2026-11-27T19:45:24.109Z","request_id":"req_011ChU1NJopBJ7WNPymHoRrq"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0181UjBBg1EEk1yREP6559qm","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (37.4KB). Full output saved to: /ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M8F8KY79SBRAGGVWYTN36CCG/0cd2a1b2-cea7-431b-b364-cc55a317e954/tool-results/bd5j2lcik.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\nok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/initcmd\t9.496s\ndocs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md:16:alternative only once #302 lands; until then use the App.\ndocs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md:100:dry-run only.\ndocs/proposal.md:74:| `ard:pending` | Scanner or human | Ready for triage; the lead's request label. On an LLM-scanner issue (route `none`) only a human sets it, and it must be `ard:pending` itself even when intake uses a custom label, because the intake sweep skips App-authored issues. Also set by the engineer's close-out in a network round, to re-run the lead |\ndocs/proposal.md:86:Eight more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:p0`, `ard:p1`, `ard:p2` and `ard:p3` (priority, set by humans only; ard never sets one: unlabelled counts as P2, several count as the highest; see the job-pool paragraph), `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on the issue and the PR, cleared by a green run), `ard:keep` (hold failed-job resources past 24 h) and `ard:pause` (on the pinned status issue, stops all new claims). `ard labels sync` creates and updates the full set from the resolved config, idempotently, and never deletes a label: stale `ard:` labels are only reported. In `label` mode a custom `intake.label` is part of the catalogue: sync creates it with a description saying it admits the issue to triage, and switching back to the default leaves the old custom label reported as stale, never deleted. `ard labels sync` and `ard config check` reject an intake label that is one of ard's own label names, naming `intake.label`; `ard serve` logs `config.intake_invalid` once per distinct value. `--repo owner/name` names the repository; when omitted, it is read from the `origin` remote. Role names are at most 31 characters and scanner names at most 38, because GitHub caps a label at 50 characters. Worker role names may not c\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"722ce48d-201b-495f-b707-ef0110c1be2a","uuid":"8af84b47-68b6-414f-beab-d551d7bc9b74","timestamp":"2026-11-27T19:45:36.916Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"ok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/initcmd\t9.496s\ndocs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md:16:alternative only once #302 lands; until then use the App.\ndocs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md:100:dry-run only.\ndocs/proposal.md:74:| `ard:pending` | Scanner or human | Ready for triage; the lead's request label. On an LLM-scanner issue (route `none`) only a human sets it, and it must be `ard:pending` itself even when intake uses a custom label, because the intake sweep skips App-authored issues. Also set by the engineer's close-out in a network round, to re-run the lead |\ndocs/proposal.md:86:Eight more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:p0`, `ard:p1`, `ard:p2` and `ard:p3` (priority, set by humans only; ard never sets one: unlabelled counts as P2, several count as the highest; see the job-pool paragraph), `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on the issue and the PR, cleared by a green run), `ard:keep` (hold failed-job resources past 24 h) and `ard:pause` (on the pinned status issue, stops all new claims). `ard labels sync` creates and updates the full set from the resolved config, idempotently, and never deletes a label: stale `ard:` labels are only reported. In `label` mode a custom `intake.label` is part of the catalogue: sync creates it with a description saying it admits the issue to triage, and switching back to the default leaves the old custom label reported as stale, never deleted. `ard labels sync` and `ard config check` reject an intake label that is one of ard's own label names, naming `intake.label`; `ard serve` logs `config.intake_invalid` once per distinct value. `--repo owner/name` names the repository; when omitted, it is read from the `origin` remote. Role names are at most 31 characters and scanner names at most 38, because GitHub caps a label at 50 characters. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so branch names stay unambiguous.\ndocs/proposal.md:120:**Repo context docs.** `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md` and `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` give agents the background a repository's code does not state. They are human-owned: no agent writes them, the worker guard rejects any change under `.ard/`, and `ard init` proposes them only as templates. Each is optional. A file that is missing, or that still holds only the template (headings and comments), counts as absent and is not shown to agents. Precedence, highest first: the role's own prompt and `.ard/config.yaml`, then the issue and its plan, then these docs; they inform a decision and never override the issue's ask. A reviewer reads them from the pull request's head, so a PR that edits one is judged against its own edit, and because the three files are protected paths that edit waits for a human approval. When a doc conflicts with the issue or with another doc, the agent does not pick a side: it reports the conflict in its role file and answers `needs-human`. Treat their content as facts about the project, not as instructions that change an agent's rules. Each template carries a size guidance of about 150 lines and a `Last reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD` line for the human to keep current.\ndocs/proposal.md:135:**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. The bounce is on in `serve` (`servecmd` sets `closeout.Deps.Network`) and applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): nothing is pushed and no PR is opened, and close-out requests `ard:triage:engineer` instead of `needs-human` and removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running`, in that order. The triage job gets `network-request.md`, rendered from the record's structured list and marked as agent-written, untrusted input. One network round per issue is allowed, counted from worker close-outs with outcome `needs-network` in any phase from prepared on (the plan never changes after Prepare, so a crash cannot make a round read as not open); a second, a fix, review or conflict round, a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human`, with one comment that lists each entry with its URL in a code span. The same applies when the bounce is off. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies; a test drives the whole path (bounce, re-run, gate, approval, worker, reviewers and the three negative cases) against fake agents with no network.\ndocs/proposal.md:137:**Network round re-run (#386).** A round is open while the newest bounced worker has no triage engineer `done` after it (job ID order). `Prepare` persists the round in the job record (`NetworkState.Round`, `Done`, `Rerun`), so `Build` stays pure and Resume replays the same plan. The engineer's first `done` in a round consumes it: its close-out clears `ard:triage:lead:done` (`Plan.Clear`, removed in the request phase before the requests are added, 404 counting as success) and adds `ard:pending`, so the lead runs first, and persists the other triage roles that had finished `done` (`Plan.Rerun`). The lead's close-out in that round re-requests those roles and clears their `:done` (roles no longer configured are skipped), so all finish before the gate. A human re-request of the engineer sees no open round and does not re-arm again. The engineer may declare `inputs:` (`path`, `provider` `human` or `worker`, optional https `url`), parsed from the redacted role file and stored in the plan. The gate checks each path (plain characters, clean and relative, not under `.ard/`, `.github/` or `.git`), reads main once per tick at one SHA through `ls-tree` (no content, regular blobs only, size capped, cached per SHA) and holds with a \"waiting for inputs\" comment listing each missing path and the capture-note format until every human-provided input exists. A `worker` input with a `network: false` worker role, a bad path or an oversized file ends `needs-human`. A network round always requires a fresh approval, even with `require_approval: false` and even when the engineer removed every network need: the \"ready for approval\" marker gains a `round=<n>`, and an earlier approval is stale because the engineer's new `:done` follows it. An approval given while inputs are missing still counts once they land. Label state machine: the engineer's `done` close-out removes `ard:triage:lead:done` and adds `ard:pending` before its own `:done`; the lead's close-out removes `:done` from each re-opened role and requests it again; the gate does not run while the lead's `:done` is absent. The gate records one `wait` gate event when it starts waiting for inputs and one `inputs-ready` event when they land; the daily status-issue digest shows a *Waiting for inputs* count of open issues whose newest gate event is the wait.\ndocs/proposal.md:141:Close-out checks `plan.md` against the job's base commit, but the committer writes it onto the latest `main`. So at commit time the committer replaces only the job's own `##` section in the current `main` copy (adding it at the end if absent, dropping it if the job removed it) and keeps every other section as `main` has it; a section another role committed after the job's base is not reverted. This runs again on each push retry. If the `main` copy cannot be merged (the job's heading appears twice, or its section holds a heading-like line), the job fails closed as `needs-human` with a `plan.md conflict` reason; like an allowlist denial, only the redacted log and the job's usage record are committed, never `plan.md` or the job's other files.\ndocs/proposal.md:154:verdict: approve      # reviewers only: approve | changes (required when status is done)\ndocs/proposal.md:184:**One committer for main.** (Concurrency rule, #411: the clone's lock is exclusive for worktree add/remove/prune and shared for fetches, so a lane's fetch never reads a half-added or half-removed worktree; commits and pushes take neither.) Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (a GH006 protected-branch rejection counts among these only when `main` moved since the lease, a lost race that is re-fetched and replayed; with `main` unchanged it is a protection failure, cause `push_protected`, not retried, and a branch-lane GH006 is never retried; re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init --pr`'s (local mode writes only the working tree, #303): it commits only `.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/ard-init.lock` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\ndocs/proposal.md:209:**Stops that are not runs.** An LLM scanner skipped because no model credential is set (`scanner.skip reason=no_model_credential`; `ard serve` then builds no agent engine, as in the spark pod's `--loops scanners,...`), skipped because the executor cannot run it (`executor: k8s` until #454: `reason=executor_unsupported`), or stopped by a usage limit (outcome `dropped_limited`, log `scanner.limited`) is not recorded: the scanner stays due, and the loop moves on to the next due scanner, so it never blocks a due `cve`. The loop checks the engine before it reads the scanner's state or GitHub, and warns once per scanner and reason, then logs at debug. A run interrupted by a controller restart does count: at startup the stale scanner job is failed and a `scan_failed` run is recorded from the job's creation time, so an LLM scanner does not spend again at once (deterministic scanners get the same record). Known leaks, documented rather than fixed: a pause mid-scan spends and files nothing; `--dry-run` spends real tokens, because the executor is not an effect (the spark dry-run pod has no credential, so it skips); an interrupted run's partial usage is not parsed at restart; scanner spend counts toward the daily caps (stage `scanner`) but has no row in any issue's cost table; and one LLM scan holds the scanners loop for up to its timeout, so `cve` waits behind it.\ndocs/proposal.md:234:**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity (issues filed under `route: none` or `needs-human` lack `ard:pending` and are never candidates; #204 may widen this) for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. A priority label does not make an issue a non-candidate. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\ndocs/proposal.md:258:**The lts scanner** (`scanners/lts`, a `sh` script plus two `jq` programs and `yq` for Helm, no build step) flags components at or near end of life using the endoflife.date v1 API (`/api/v1/products/<slug>`, one request per referenced product). A component is a product plus a release cycle. Its fingerprint is `eol:<product>:<cycle>` (for example `eol:nodejs:18`), one finding lists every referencing file, and `group` is the product. The human-owned `products.json` maps Docker image names and a few npm packages to endoflife.date slugs (the Alpine image maps to `alpine-linux`); names not in it are skipped with a stderr line. Cycles match generically: a codename's first word (`bookworm`, `jammy`), else the longest dotted prefix of the version. It reads the `toolchain` line (else the `go` directive) of `go.mod`; `engines.node` and table npm dependencies of `package.json` (lowest lower bound of the range); Dockerfile `FROM` lines (`Dockerfile`, `Dockerfile.*`, `*.Dockerfile`, `Containerfile`; global `ARG` substitution, registry and digest stripped); and `repository`/`tag` or `image: name:tag` in the `values.yaml` beside each `Chart.yaml`. Severity is `high` at or past end of life, `medium` within 90 days, `low` within 180 days, otherwise no finding. A component referenced only by floors (a `go` directive, `engines.node`, an npm range) is capped at `medium`. Titles carry the date (`Go 1.21: end of life 2024-08-13`), never \"approaching\" or \"past\", because rerate changes only severity. The scan fails closed with nothing on stdout, because an absent finding closes its issue: any API failure (including a 404 for a mapped product, which means `products.json` is stale), a non-JSON response, and a `package.json` or `values.yaml` that cannot be parsed. A missing tool exits 2 (`yq` only when a `Chart.yaml` exists). Paths with a `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` or `testdata` segment are skipped. Manifest text reaches the issue body only as cleaned text in code spans (control characters and backticks removed, 200 characters, at most 50 references), and repo text never reaches a URL.\ndocs/proposal.md:260:**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `SecretParts`, `ExtraData` and `StructuredData` stay in pipes inside `scan`. The fingerprint is `trufflehog:` plus the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 over `ard-trufflehog-v1`, a NUL byte and the base64 of the secret (an unsalted hash, so one value gives one issue wherever it appears). The scan fails closed: a non-zero trufflehog exit, a missing `finished scanning` summary, zero chunks on a non-empty tree, or a result count that differs from the summary produces no findings, so nothing is autoclosed on a partial run. `exclude-paths` (regexes; `.git` is always excluded) and `allow` (one fingerprint per line) are human-owned; a bad `allow` entry fails the run. Findings use `route: needs-human`, severity high, and carry a runbook that says to rotate the credential first. `commit` is the scanned SHA, recorded in `.ard/<n>/issue.md`; history is scanned by `scanners/trufflehog-history`. The forbidden flags (`--since-commit`, `--max-depth`, `--detector-timeout`, `--archive-timeout`, `--filter-unverified`, `--filter-entropy`, `--results`) are never passed, because a partial or load-dependent result would autoclose live findings. Caveats: (1) a secret gone from HEAD is not revoked; autoclose closes the issue as completed, so the issue body tells the human to rotate first. (2) The fingerprint hash is unsalted, an offline oracle for a low-entropy value that is already in the repo at HEAD; accepted. (3) Issues on a public repo show `file:line`. (4) At `max_open` the scanner is skipped, so new leaks go unseen until a human resolves one; scanner failure streaks are posted on the status issue (#221). (5) Any future change that commits scanner logs must exclude this scanner. (6) On a public repo with GitHub secret scanning off, this scanner is the only detection channel, and it cannot stop a push. TruffleHog is AGPL-3.0: it is installed by the role's Dockerfile (or a host install) and is not in the published `ard` image or the root `Dockerfile`. Host and Docker integration tests (`go test -tags integration -run Trufflehog ./internal/roles/`, with `ARD_TRUFFLEHOG_BIN` at the Dockerfile's pinned version) prove the role against a real binary and image.\ndocs/proposal.md:274:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is edited after admission, the issue is re-gated with `ard:needs-human` and a comment until a maintainer re-applies the intake label. App-authored issues are exempt from the hash check. The loop never waits for a job: each claimed job is handed to a watcher goroutine that closes out through the shared Finisher, and a restart leaves running jobs to pool adoption and the Reaper. The re-gate hash covers the title and the body without ard's own cost block (see Guardrails, \"Intake is configurable\"), so ard's table writes never re-gate an issue. The first time ard writes the block, the stored hash stays valid; a maintainer relabel that falls between a human edit and ard's next table write may need repeating, because an ard body write bumps the issue's `updated_at`, which fails safe.\ndocs/proposal.md:292:Before it asks for approval or starts a worker, the gate checks that `.ard/<n>/plan.md` exists on `main`. If it does not, the gate posts one comment, adds `ard:needs-human` and records a `gate` event with reason `no-plan`; a check that fails to run is retried on the next tick. Removing a role's request label by hand does not count as that role finishing. With `require_approval: true` (the Phase 1 default) the gate posts one \"ready for approval\" comment per lead job and waits. `ard:approved` is honoured only when its latest `labeled` event (reset by an `unlabeled`) is by a collaborator with triage permission or higher, is not the App, and comes after the last triage `:done` label of the issue. An approval given earlier is left in place and ignored: the gate waits for a fresh one and does not escalate. In a network round the gate first checks the engineer's `inputs:`: a human-provided path missing from `main` holds it with one \"waiting for inputs\" comment, and when the files land it posts a fresh \"ready for approval\" with a new `round=<n>` marker, even with `require_approval: false`; an earlier approval is stale because the engineer's new `:done` follows it, so a maintainer removes and re-adds `ard:approved`. A `worker` input with a `network: false` worker role, a bad path or an oversized file ends `needs-human`. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor. In `ard serve`'s terminal UI the `a` key on a queued issue asks for confirmation, then runs the operator's own `gh issue edit --add-label ard:approved` (or opens the issue URL when `gh` is missing or logged out) and wakes the triage loop; the App never adds `ard:approved`, and under `--dry-run` the call is recorded in the ledger, not run. A human who adds `ard:worker:<role>` directly bypasses the gate and has approved by doing so. The gate does not enforce that `triage:engineer` ran: the lead routes and the gate applies its routing. In `--dry-run` the gate logs `triage.would_gate` and writes nothing; otherwise each action records a `gate` event.\ndocs/proposal.md:307:**Sidecar repos are context, read-only.** A role's sidecar (for example `vpe-specs`) is cloned by the executor, not the loop: the loop passes the role's resolved sidecar (repo and ref) on the job. The executor keeps one shallow (depth 1) bare object cache per sidecar repo at `~/.ard/sidecars/<controlled-owner>/<controlled-repo>/<sidecar-owner>/<sidecar-repo>.git`, shared by every job of that controlled repo, and exports the commit into the job home as `<job home>/sidecar` (plain files: no `.git`, `chmod -R a-w` in both modes, and a `:ro` mount under Docker). The job sees it at `$ARD_SIDECAR`. `ref` is a branch, tag or full 40-character SHA (an abbreviated SHA is a config error). A SHA already in the cache needs no network; a branch or tag is re-fetched (depth 1) for every job. Whatever the ref, `Prepare` resolves it to a full commit SHA and records it on the job's handle (and in the `executor.sidecar_ready` log), so a job is reproducible. Symlinks and submodules in the sidecar are rejected and the tree is capped at 64 MiB, like config snapshots. A fetch failure (network, auth, a stale lock, which the executor clears by rebuilding the cache once) is the transient `clone_failed`; a ref or SHA that does not exist, or a repo the App cannot read, is a permanent failure that names the sidecar. A role that wants a spec changed says so in `plan.md`; editing the sidecar is out of scope for v1.\ndocs/proposal.md:313:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, exclusively, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands and a fetch aborts if it reads a worktree entry that is half added or removed (#411); fetches take the same lock shared (they still run in parallel), and commits and pushes take neither. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. A scanner job may also ask for an output directory (`Job.Output`, scanner stage only; any other stage is `ErrInvalidJob`): `Prepare` creates an empty `<job home>/out` (0700), the job sees it at `$ARD_OUT` (`Handle.View.Out`; the host path in host mode, a writable bind mount at `/out` under Docker while the worktree stays `:ro`), and it is removed with the job home. It is how an LLM scanner hands back its findings file (#204); the Kubernetes executor refuses an `Output` job until its pod has such a mount (#454), so `ard serve` under `executor: k8s` skips LLM scanners without recording a run (`scanner.skip reason=executor_unsupported`) instead of failing each one at prepare. A scanner agent job is bounded by the executor's hard timeout and the role's `max_budget_usd`; the Reaper leaves scanner-stage jobs to the scanner tick. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Under `executor: k8s` the `pre` container instead gets a per-job installation token scoped to contents:read on the job's repositories (`github.AppAuth.MintReadToken`), revoked when the job ends; a personal access token cannot be scoped down, so that executor needs GitHub App auth. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. **Default agent image under the local executor (#244, part of #55).** `ard serve` and `ard config check` read the host setting `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`: a digest-pinned reference (`<name>@sha256:<64 hex>`) or a local image ID (`sha256:<hex>` from `make agent-image`); tags are refused (`executor.CheckImageRef`, `ErrImageRef`), and an invalid value stops startup. When it is set, `Prepare` runs every job that reads untrusted input and has no `Dockerfile` (triage, worker agent, test and fix steps, reviewer, and LLM scanner jobs) under Docker in that image, with the same flags and mounts as a Dockerfile role (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, worktree at `/work`, role directory `:ro`, and so on). A role's own `Dockerfile` wins. Deterministic scanners (trust","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M8F8KY79SBRAGGVWYTN36CCG/0cd2a1b2-cea7-431b-b364-cc55a317e954/tool-results/bd5j2lcik.txt","persistedOutputSize":38345},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0181UjBBg1EEk1yREP6559qm","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
